      <div class="case-content">
        <section id="kv-epoch">
          <h2>1. Quadratic multi-turn cost &amp; KV-Cache Epoch Step-Function Law</h2>
          <p>
            In an autoregressive agent session with static system/tool prefix <code>S₀</code> and per-turn tool observations <code>oₜ</code> over <code>T</code> turns, cumulative input token volume scales quadratically:
          </p>
          <pre><code>C_input(T) = T · S₀ + Σ(t=1..T) (T - t + 1) · oₜ</code></pre>
          <p>
            A widespread anti-pattern is retroactive sliding-window pruning—mutating a past observation <code>B</code> at turn <code>k &lt; t</code> down to <code>αB</code>. Because causal Transformer KV-caches are prefix-hashed, altering a single byte at <code>k</code> invalidates the entire subsequent suffix <code>P_after(k)</code>. With cached-token price ratio <code>c ∈ (0, 1)</code> (e.g. <code>c = 0.10</code> for 90% cache discount), retroactive pruning yields positive net savings only if the remaining conversation horizon exceeds:
          </p>
          <pre><code>H* = ceil( ((1 - c) · (P_after(k) + αB)) / (c · (1 - α) · B) )</code></pre>
          <p>
            <strong>Numerical proof:</strong> for <code>c = 0.10</code>, <code>α = 0.20</code>, <code>B = 2,000</code> tokens, and <code>P_after(k) = 15,000</code> tokens, <strong><code>H* = ceil(13,860 / 160) = 87 turns</code></strong>. Compressing an observation 3 turns back loses money for the next 86 turns. This justifies Cockpit's strictly append-only native session rule in <code>compact_context.rs</code>, deferring compaction to full epoch rebases.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="speculative">
          <h2>2. Speculative turn elimination (<code>ΔT = -1</code>)</h2>
          <p>
            When the MCP server speculatively inlines ≤ 24 lines of an exact symbol definition (cost <code>B_inline</code>) on a narrow <code>fs_search</code> hit, eliminating the follow-up <code>fs_read_range</code> round-trip saves net tokens whenever the transition probability <code>p = P(Read | Search)</code> satisfies:
          </p>
          <pre><code>p &gt; τ* = B_inline / (C_ctx(t) + T_out + B_inline)</code></pre>
          <p>
            At <code>C_ctx(t) = 25,000</code> and <code>B_inline = 180</code> tokens, <strong><code>τ* = 0.71%</code></strong>—meaning eliminating a single round-trip yields orders of magnitude more savings than compressing an individual message.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="bpe">
          <h2>3. BPE geometry: bijective aliasing &amp; strided line anchors</h2>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Hexadecimal fragmentation in regex pre-tokenizers:</strong> splitting digits (<code>\p{'{N}'}{'{1,3}'}</code>) from letters (<code>\p{'{L}'}+</code>) induces a per-step split probability of <code>P_split = 2 · (10/16) · (6/16) = 0.46875</code> on uniform hex strings (~19.5 tokens per UUID/SHA-1).</li>
            <li><strong>Reversible MCP channel aliasing (<code>Φ / Φ⁻¹</code>):</strong> replacing repeated entities (<code>k ≥ 2</code>) with short tokens (<code>$U1</code>, <code>$H1</code>, <code>$P1</code>) in tool outputs and expanding <code>Φ⁻¹</code> on incoming tool arguments saves <code>(k - 1)·τ(e) - 2k - 3</code> tokens losslessly.</li>
            <li><strong>Strided line-number quantization:</strong> emitting line numbers every <code>S = 10</code> lines and on declaration headers cuts line-prefix token overhead by <code>1 - 1/S = 90.0%</code>.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="algebra">
          <h2>4. Program slicing, diagnostic <code>δ-CRDT</code> &amp; Patricia-Trie</h2>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Weiser intra-procedural slicing (Knaster-Tarski fixed point):</strong> iteratively computing backward data/control dependencies over <code>DEF(s)</code>, <code>USE(s)</code>, and <code>CTRL(s)</code> converges in ≤ 6 passes in pure Rust to isolate causal statements for a target variable.</li>
            <li><strong>Line-shift-invariant diagnostic diffing (<code>δ-CRDT</code>):</strong> projecting compiler diagnostics to <code>(kind, code, file, FNV1a64(stripped_msg))</code> lets subsequent build checks emit only <code>(Δ⁻ resolved, Δ⁺ new, |Dₜ ∩ Dₜ₋₁|)</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Patricia-Trie path factorization:</strong> factoring shared directory prefixes and extensions (<code>dir/{'{a,b,c}'}.rs</code>) compresses repository trees losslessly.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="stochastic">
          <h2>5. Absorbing Markov chains &amp; Adaptive Group Testing</h2>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Trajectory cost forecasting &amp; early abort:</strong> modeling agent tool loops as an absorbing Markov chain with transient states <code>{'{Search, Skeleton, Read, Patch, Check}'}</code> and absorbing states <code>{'{Success, Fail}'}</code> yields the 5×5 fundamental matrix <code>N = (I - Q)⁻¹</code> (microsecond Gauss-Jordan), giving expected remaining token cost <code>(N·c)ᵢ</code> and success probability <code>B = N·R</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Adaptive Group Testing (Dorfman / binary splitting):</strong> locating <code>d</code> faulty tests among <code>N</code> independent tests with monotone OR-group runs requires at most <code>d·ceil(log₂(N/d)) + d - 1</code> runs (7 runs for 1 failure in 128 tests) vs. <code>N</code> linear runs.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

